    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 6884876855,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-a71c9ca70aab",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 570.0006865894824,
      "y": -2013.1255637180348,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 567.2392578125,
      "height": 227.5,
      "seed": 442873,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Core vs Platform\ndbt Core is the open-source command-line framework and runtime: you bring the local\nenvironment, adapter packages, `profiles.yml`, orchestrator, artifact storage, docs hosting,\ncredentials, and operational monitoring. The dbt platform, formerly dbt Cloud, wraps dbt\nexecution in a managed product surface: browser or CLI development, managed\nenvironments, connection profiles, credentials, jobs, CI, scheduling, docs/catalog, alerts,\nmetadata APIs, and features such as deferral and Mesh workflows that depend on shared\nproduction metadata.\n\nThe project code can be mostly the same in both, but the execution context is different. In\nCore, a command resolves context from local files, flags, env vars, and your orchestrator; in\nthe dbt platform, the context usually comes from configured environments, jobs, developer\nor deployment credentials, platform-managed artifacts, and repository integrations.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-a71c9ca70aab",
      "originalText": "Core vs Platform\ndbt Core is the open-source command-line framework and runtime: you bring the local environment, adapter packages, `profiles.yml`, orchestrator, artifact storage, docs hosting, credentials, and operational monitoring. The dbt platform, formerly dbt Cloud, wraps dbt execution in a managed product surface: browser or CLI development, managed environments, connection profiles, credentials, jobs, CI, scheduling, docs/catalog, alerts, metadata APIs, and features such as deferral and Mesh workflows that depend on shared production metadata.\n\nThe project code can be mostly the same in both, but the execution context is different. In Core, a command resolves context from local files, flags, env vars, and your orchestrator; in the dbt platform, the context usually comes from configured environments, jobs, developer or deployment credentials, platform-managed artifacts, and repository integrations.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "dbt-root-core-vs-platform"
      }
    },
