      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Profile\nA profile is the named connection context that tells dbt how to connect to a warehouse. It keeps deployment and\nruntime connection details out of transformation SQL, but Core and the dbt platform store this context differently.\n\nIn local Core or Fusion CLI, `profile:` in `dbt_project.yml` names a profile in `profiles.yml`, and `--profile` can\noverride it. Example: `profile: analytics` points to an `analytics:` block with Snowflake, BigQuery, or Databricks\noutputs.\n\nIn the dbt platform, projects do not require `profiles.yml` unless you develop from a local machine. Platform profiles\nare UI/API-managed objects that define connections, credentials, and attributes for deployment environments;\ndevelopment environments use individual developer credentials instead.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-38d7743c90b3",
      "originalText": "Profile\nA profile is the named connection context that tells dbt how to connect to a warehouse. It keeps deployment and runtime connection details out of transformation SQL, but Core and the dbt platform store this context differently.\n\nIn local Core or Fusion CLI, `profile:` in `dbt_project.yml` names a profile in `profiles.yml`, and `--profile` can override it. Example: `profile: analytics` points to an `analytics:` block with Snowflake, BigQuery, or Databricks outputs.\n\nIn the dbt platform, projects do not require `profiles.yml` unless you develop from a local machine. Platform profiles are UI/API-managed objects that define connections, credentials, and attributes for deployment environments; development environments use individual developer credentials instead.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "dbt-lifecycle-profile"
      }
    },
